---
// biome-ignore lint/correctness/noUnusedImports: Astro uses this layout component below.
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="About | plansplease"
  description="plansplease gives the useful pages made by coding agents a place to land."
  activePath="about"
>
  <section class="page-heading" aria-labelledby="about-title">
    <div>
      <h1 id="about-title">A place for the useful things agents make.</h1>
      <p>
        plansplease solves a simple problem: AI agents like Claude Code create HTML because Markdown
        is too limited, but viewing and sharing those HTML files has too much friction.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="about-content">
    <div class="about-narrative">
      <h2>The Shift from Markdown to HTML</h2>
      <p>
        For years, Markdown was the default format for LLM outputs. But as coding agents like
        Claude Code take on deeper architectural planning, Markdown breaks down. A 500-line Markdown
        spec is painful to navigate, lacks visual hierarchy, and cannot represent interactive
        explorations.
      </p>
      <p>
        HTML solves this. An agent can generate interactive architectural specs with SVG diagrams,
        PR reviews with inline annotations, UI prototypes with live sliders, and throwaway data editors
        with JSON export buttons.
      </p>
      <p>
        The friction is how to view them. Opening an HTML file directly from disk triggers browser
        CORS security blocks and breaks relative assets. Spinning up an ad-hoc HTTP server or
        deploying a repository takes longer than prompting the tool itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        plansplease eliminates that friction:
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="values-grid">
      <article class="value-card">
        <h3>Instant local previews</h3>
        <p>
          The CLI daemon runs on your machine with zero configuration. It serves files over loopback,
          binds a private port, and automatically opens your default browser.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="value-card">
        <h3>A cloud copy that stays</h3>
        <p>
          Save a cloud copy to your workspace alongside the local URL. Revisit your agent's
          interactive specs and tools long after the chat session has ended. Cloud files have
          public URLs during alpha.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="value-card">
        <h3>Pure web standards</h3>
        <p>
          No proprietary bundler lock-in. Full support for standard HTML5, SVG diagrams, Canvas,
          inline Tailwind, and multi-file asset folders.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="value-card">
        <h3>Open source foundation</h3>
        <p>
          The core daemon, CLI, and storage engine are 100% open source on
          <a href="https://github.com/abijith-suresh/plansplease">GitHub</a>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
  .about-content {
    padding: 3.5rem 0 5rem;
  }

  .about-narrative {
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin-bottom: 3.5rem;
  }

  .about-narrative h2 {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    color: var(--text-white);
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    letter-spacing: -0.022em;
  }

  .about-narrative p {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    color: var(--night-muted);
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }


  .values-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1.75rem;
  }

  .value-card {
    padding: 2rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--night-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--night-card);
  }

  .value-card h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    color: var(--text-white);
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .value-card p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--night-muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }


  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .values-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
